{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"prenatal-maudlin-seahorse","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## The gap\n\nrebar ALREADY detects duplicate creation. `create_ticket` returns a `duplicate_warning` when another\nticket with the same normalized title was created inside a recency window\n(`src/rebar/_lib_writes.py:182`, surfaced through `types.py:264` and `_mcp_writes.py:182`).\n\nBut it is **advisory and per-call**. `_warn_advisory` hands the string back to the caller and that is\nthe end of it. Nothing counts these, nothing publishes a metric, nothing alarms. So an automated\nwriter creating hundreds of duplicates emits hundreds of individual advisories that no human reads,\nand the situation is invisible until someone looks at Jira by eye.\n\nThat is what happened on 2026-09-05: an operator noticed hundreds of open Jira issues including\nduplicates. Nothing in the monitoring surface had said a word.\n\n## Why detect in rebar rather than Jira — this is the load-bearing design point\n\nJira-side detection is bounded by ACLI's per-query ceiling (1,200 issues; bug `f6cc`), which is\nexactly the constraint whose workaround is the leading suspect for causing this incident. A detector\nthat inherits that ceiling can be blinded by the same defect it exists to catch.\n\nrebar's store has no such limit. Duplicate creation is observable **locally, in the event log**,\nwith no Jira call at all. So the detector is immune to query-size limits, ACLI availability, and Jira\nlatency.\n\n## The detector must be independent of the bridge\n\nDo NOT emit this metric from the reconciler/bridge itself. A bridge that is malfunctioning must not\nbe the thing responsible for reporting that it is malfunctioning — that is a dead-man inversion, and\nthis repo has already paid for that class (`herbal-garish-monkey`, `illbred-sour-alpaca`). The\nobserver must be able to see the store and publish regardless of whether any reconciliation ran.\n\nNote the bridge runs in GitHub Actions (`.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml`), NOT on the host,\nso a host-side observer reading the store is already independent of it by construction.\n\n## Do not repeat this month's alarm defects\n\nTwo live bugs describe exactly how to get this wrong, and the new alarm must not inherit either:\n\n- `undefiled-revivable-gemsbok` — `pct_of_cap` clamps at 100, so cap-compliance metrics cannot\n  report the breach they exist to detect. Do not clamp a duplicate count or rate.\n- `illbred-sour-alpaca` — `datapoints_to_alarm = 2` of `evaluation_periods = 3` with\n  `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`, against a publisher whose cadence drifts, produces alarms that\n  fire on healthy operation AND, when the publisher is slow, alarms that can never clear. Choose the\n  missing-data policy and M-of-N deliberately for THIS metric's real publish cadence and justify the\n  choice in the change.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A metric counts duplicate ticket creations observed in the rebar store over a window, derived\n      from the SAME normalized-title rule `duplicate_warning` already uses — reuse that rule, do not\n      invent a second definition that can disagree with it.\n- [ ] The metric is published by an observer that does NOT depend on the bridge running, and this is\n      demonstrated: with reconciliation stopped, the metric still publishes.\n- [ ] A CloudWatch alarm fires on a RATE consistent with mass duplication and not on ordinary\n      operation. State the threshold and justify it against real numbers — a handful of same-title\n      tickets is legitimate; hundreds in an hour is not. Include the observed count from the\n      2026-09-05 incident in the justification.\n- [ ] The alarm's `treat_missing_data`, `evaluation_periods` and `datapoints_to_alarm` are chosen\n      against this publisher's measured cadence, with the reasoning recorded. Explicitly state why the\n      combination cannot reproduce `illbred-sour-alpaca`'s false-page or unclearable-alarm behaviour.\n- [ ] No clamping anywhere in the metric path.\n- [ ] Defect-seeded: synthesise a burst of same-normalized-title creations in a test store, confirm\n      the metric rises and the alarm condition is met; remove the burst and confirm it clears. An\n      alarm never observed firing is not evidence.\n- [ ] Replay proof: run the detector against the store history covering 2026-09-05 05:27-09:26 UTC\n      (the five long reconciler passes in the incident window) and show it would have fired. If it\n      would NOT have fired, the threshold or the signal is wrong — say so rather than adjusting the\n      criterion.\n- [ ] Portability: local store read plus host metric publish; no dependence on any single CI provider\n      trigger.\n\n## Testing\n\n1. RED first: with the detector absent, show the incident window produces no signal.\n2. Unit: the normalized-title rule matches `duplicate_warning`'s, proven by driving both on the same\n   inputs rather than by reading the code.\n3. Replay: the historical window above.\n4. Mutation: break the aggregation and confirm a test turns RED.\n\n## Provenance\n\nRequested by the operator during the 2026-09-05 duplicate-reconciliation incident, as prevention\nrather than remediation: \"even with limited Jira query size, we should be able to detect the creation\nof multiple duplicates in rebar and alarm on the situation.\" The incident RCA is separate; this\nticket is the detection gap it exposed, and is worth landing even if the RCA refutes its prime\nsuspect.","id":"dbe7-d957-e5da-49dd","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Duplicate ticket creation is detectable in rebar but nothing aggregates or alarms on it, so a reconciler creating hundreds of duplicates is silent"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788621079663951045,"uuid":"09520870-72ee-431a-bb6d-4439fa31c68f"}